g the native patient cells then you  can see variation and I think some of  that can come from the patient  themselves with the disease they have a  lot of it can come from the method  that's used to generate those IPS cells  so I think that overcome that I think  patient derived cells are very important  but I would certainly advocate the use  of precise genome editing technologies  to make isogenic pairs of those cells  and by that I mean so either taking a  native cell and mutating the gene of  interest or taking your patient cell and  then mutating if it's a mononuclear  disease that gene back and i think there  you have the perfect control because  it's isagenix so the only thing that's  changed is the G's you've changed  I appreciate with a lot  diseases they're not monogenic and so  that's difficult to apply but I think if  you can get isogenic controls perfect if  you're going from patient derive  material I think what you will need to  do is you would need to have a number of  cells from the patient so both from the  same patient maybe of nr.3 from the same  patient and also from different patients  to show that your results are  reproducible across a number of lines  from a number of patients I think that's  what you would need to do okay we have  somebody would like to know if your your  organoids can be expanded indefinitely  or do you need to continuously  reinitiate them from your IPS C's s so  they cannot be grown indefinitely so we  we can take them out so we've shown them  that we can culture them so I guess they  go into organ wood formation on day 12  we've taken them to day 50 you do see  changes over that time course and you do  see maturation but it's not indefinite  culture you will see deterioration after  a while and so we we have routine  generations so every every couple of  weeks we will start a new round of  generating these organoids okay from the  the drug discovery work that you've  shown with wondered if there's any  evidence to date